# Line endings are part of correctness here, not cosmetics.
#
# Measured 2026-10-08: with core.autocrlf=true and no .gitattributes,
# scripts/diagnose_host.sh was stored in git with CRLF on all 204 lines. A bash
# script with CRLF endings fails on Linux (`/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file
# or directory`, and `$'\r': command not found` on every line) - so it would have
# failed on the very host it was written for. Normalize in the REPOSITORY so the
# bytes a Linux clone receives are correct regardless of each machine's config.

# Shell scripts must be LF. Non-negotiable: CRLF breaks the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Sources and config: normalize to LF in the repository; git converts on
# checkout only where a platform wants it.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# The committed sample database is binary. Never let EOL conversion touch it:
# a single flipped byte would corrupt the SQLite file. It is also a build
# artefact - `python db/build_db.py` regenerates it byte-for-byte.
*.db    binary
*.db-wal binary
*.db-shm binary
*.zip   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.woff2 binary